{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OPERATOR DECISION (2026-08-07): SPLIT THE POSTURE BY LANE.\n\nA transitive-dependency advisory should:\n* **BLOCK the scheduled CI run on main** — main is where a known-vulnerable pinned dependency must surface loudly and get fixed. The scheduled run is the right owner because the trigger is the world changing, not anyone's change.\n* **NOT block Gerrit verify** — UNLESS the change under review actually touches the dependency in question.\n\nRationale: the current failure mode punishes the wrong people. When click 8.2.1 / PYSEC-2026-2132 landed, six changes across five work streams went red simultaneously and each author independently began diagnosing an advisory none of them caused or could fix. Advisory triage belongs to whoever owns the lock, on a lane that watches main — not to every author with a change in flight.\n\nImplementation implications (for whoever takes this):\n* the Gerrit verify lane needs to determine whether a change TOUCHES the dependency in question. The obvious signal is whether the change modifies uv.lock / pyproject dependency declarations; decide whether that is sufficient or whether a finer package-level comparison against the advisory's package is wanted. State the choice — do not leave it implicit.\n* the scheduled main lane must FAIL VISIBLY and route to an owner. A scheduled red that nobody is paged for reproduces the current problem in a quieter form. Consider filing a ticket automatically, matching how the heartbeat canary escalates.\n* both lanes keep using pip-audit as-is: it correctly refuses to distinguish 'reachable in this project' from 'present in the environment', and this decision does NOT ask it to start guessing reachability. The lane split is the mechanism, not reachability analysis.\n* the retry behaviour stays as documented in this ticket — only DB-UNREACHABLE failures are recheckable — but the Gerrit-lane message should say so, since 'recheck' being everyone's first instinct is the single most time-wasting property recorded here.\n\nThe banked uv knowledge in this ticket (constraint-dependencies can only NARROW and returns UNSATISFIABLE as expected output; override-dependencies is the correct instrument; uv lock --upgrade-package is a no-op against an upstream cap) stays valid and must survive into whatever documentation the fix produces."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786098465417277001,"uuid":"d91739a9-0f9f-4c43-aace-73c1daebf85c"}